            "body": "Resolves #1558\n\nA `redirect` rule no longer fires when the request path already has the shape of its target, so a target inside its own pattern doesn't loop and doesn't need a `false` reset:\n\n```ts\nrouteRules({\n  \"/docs/**\": { redirect: \"/docs/v2/**\" }, // /docs/v2/** is left alone\n  \"GET:MD /blog/**\": { redirect: \"/blog/**.md\" }, // any /blog/**.md is left alone\n});\n```\n\n### Shape rules (`src/rules/handlers/_at-target.ts`)\n\nThe shape is worked out once when the handler is built. At request time the guard costs a `startsWith`/`endsWith` check plus the scope check.\n\n- **Trailing `/**`** (`/docs/v2/**`): skip on `/docs/v2` or anything under `/docs/v2/`.\n- **Interpolated `**`** (`/blog/**.md`): skip when the path starts with the literal before the first `**` and ends with the literal after the last one, at any depth.\n- **No `**` in the path** (`/new`, `/new?from=**`, `/app/#/**`): skip only on that exact path.\n- **Only the path of `to` is compared.** Target literals are encoded the way `event.url.pathname` is served, so `/café` matches.\n\n### Never skipped\n\n- **Absolute URL targets.** Their origin could only be compared against the client-controlled `Host`. This is stricter than the issue, which only asked that other-origin targets keep today's behavior.\n- **Degenerate shapes** such as a bare `/**`. Skipping would disable the rule entirely.\n- **A `**` in the middle of a segment** (`/docs/v2-**`).\n- **Requests the rule would reject.** The target is resolved before the skip decision, so every existing 400 (for example, a variable-width key prefix like `/:lang?/old/**`) still stands.\n- **Encoded traversals.** A wildcard shape is only honored when every canonical reading of the path stays within the target's own directory (`isPathInScope`). So `/docs/v2/..%2f..%2fadmin` still reaches the rule and gets its `400`, and is never let through to the app.\n\n### Behavior change\n\nA rule that deliberately redirected a path to another path of the same target shape (e.g. `/a/b/c` → `/a/b/b/c`) will stop firing there. This should go in the changelog.\n\n### Review\n\nAn independent subagent reviewed this. Its findings are addressed in the second commit:\n- a `**` only in the query or fragment gave a truncated prefix\n- mid-segment prefixes could be skipped past an encoded traversal\n- the check trusted `Host`\n- target literals weren't compared in their served (percent-encoded) spelling\n\nIt also flagged a pre-existing, unrelated issue: a mid-segment target such as `to: \"/docs/v2-**\"` answers 400 to every non-empty tail (`GET /docs/intro` → 400 on `main` too). That's left for a separate fix.\n\n🤖 Generated with AI assistant\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **Bug Fixes**\n  - Redirects are skipped when a request path already matches an eligible relative target, including exact-path and wildcard targets. Comparisons ignore query strings and fragments. Absolute targets and catch-all targets still redirect, as do paths outside the target’s served scope.\n- **Documentation**\n  - Clarified which target patterns qualify for redirect skipping and how wildcard paths are compared.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n",
